WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.173
You type ls into a shell, the program that reads your commands, and a moment later ls is running, listing files.

2
00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:13.433
Now add a greater-than sign and a file name.

3
00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:18.422
The same ls runs, but its list lands in the file instead of on the screen.

4
00:00:18.422 --> 00:00:19.702
And ls never knows.

5
00:00:19.702 --> 00:00:21.523
How does the shell do that?

6
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:26.516
The simplest design is one request to the kernel: spawn this program.

7
00:00:26.516 --> 00:00:28.031
It works for plain ls.

8
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.593
Now you want the output in a file, so spawn needs a setting for where output goes.

9
00:00:33.593 --> 00:00:34.820
Then one for input.

10
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.112
One for a pipe that feeds one program into another.

11
00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:43.900
One for the folder to start in, for the settings it inherits, for the user, for the limits.

12
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:49.068
Every new kind of setup becomes one more setting, and the form only ever gets longer.

13
00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:53.341
Unix took another route in the 1970s, and Linux kept it.

14
00:00:53.341 --> 00:00:58.136
Split the job into two calls, and let your own code run in between.

15
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.801
Unix used family words for this, and they fit.

16
00:01:01.801 --> 00:01:07.641
Every process has a number, its PID, and a parent: the process that started it.

17
00:01:07.641 --> 00:01:11.412
Here, the parent is the shell, tinysh, with PID 43.

18
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:13.386
The first call is fork.

19
00:01:13.386 --> 00:01:16.751
It makes a copy of the process that calls it.

20
00:01:16.751 --> 00:01:18.919
The child gets a new PID, 45.

21
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:24.763
Everything else starts as a copy of the parent: the code, the memory and the open files.

22
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.862
Here the family picture breaks.

23
00:01:26.862 --> 00:01:29.926
No real child is born with their parent's memories.

24
00:01:29.926 --> 00:01:33.169
This one is, down to the line it was in the middle of.

25
00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:36.950
Both of them come back from the same fork call.

26
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.567
The parent gets the child's PID, 45.

27
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:40.803
The child gets 0.

28
00:01:40.803 --> 00:01:43.565
That's how each one knows which it is.

29
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.544
I know what you're thinking: copying a whole process sounds slow.

30
00:01:47.544 --> 00:01:49.445
The kernel doesn't copy at first.

31
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:57.707
Parent and child share the memory, and the kernel copies a page, a 4 KB piece, only when one of them writes to it.

32
00:01:58.067 --> 00:02:00.340
Now the gap, which is the whole point.

33
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:06.144
The child still runs the shell's code, so it can change things about itself before it becomes ls.

34
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.211
A process names its open files with small numbers, file descriptors.

35
00:02:11.211 --> 00:02:16.376
0 is where input comes from, 1 is where output goes, and 2 is for errors.

36
00:02:16.633 --> 00:02:19.884
Right now, the child's 1 points at the terminal.

37
00:02:19.884 --> 00:02:25.709
So the child opens listing.txt, and points descriptor 1 at it, with a call named dup2.

38
00:02:25.709 --> 00:02:28.621
Meanwhile the parent goes to sleep in wait.

39
00:02:28.867 --> 00:02:30.624
Then the child calls exec.

40
00:02:30.624 --> 00:02:37.115
The kernel throws away the shell's code and memory inside the child, and loads ls from the disk.

41
00:02:37.367 --> 00:02:39.665
Before we look: what did ls keep?

42
00:02:39.665 --> 00:02:43.355
The PID, the parent, the open files, or none of them?

43
00:02:43.355 --> 00:02:44.469
Pause and guess.

44
00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:48.567
All three.

45
00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:56.491
It's still PID 45, its parent is still 43, and descriptor 1 still points at listing.txt.

46
00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.947
exec doesn't start a new process.

47
00:02:58.947 --> 00:03:01.738
It swaps the program inside the same one.

48
00:03:01.738 --> 00:03:05.414
ls writes its list to descriptor 1, as it always does.

49
00:03:05.414 --> 00:03:08.545
The lines land in the file, and ls never knew.

50
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.199
The third call closes the loop.

51
00:03:11.199 --> 00:03:14.759
ls ends with exit status 0, which means success.

52
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.127
The kernel frees its memory, but keeps a small record: who it was, and how it ended.

53
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.609
It wakes the parent, like a parent waiting up to hear how the evening went.

54
00:03:24.867 --> 00:03:31.807
wait hands the parent two numbers, 45 and status 0, and only then does the kernel delete the record.

55
00:03:32.067 --> 00:03:35.698
Between those two moments, the dead child has a grim name.

56
00:03:35.698 --> 00:03:37.200
That's the next section.

57
00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.298
Two rules come with this.

58
00:03:39.298 --> 00:03:43.964
A successful exec never returns, because the code that called it is gone.

59
00:03:44.233 --> 00:03:50.134
So the line after exec runs only if exec failed, and it should exit at once.

60
00:03:50.134 --> 00:03:55.724
tinysh exits with 127, the number real shells use for command not found.

61
00:03:55.967 --> 00:03:58.855
And a parent should always wait for its children.

62
00:03:58.855 --> 00:04:04.279
That's where the shell's dollar question mark, and Java's Process.waitFor, get their number.

63
00:04:04.533 --> 00:04:08.809
So how did the shell put ls's list in a file, without ls knowing?

64
00:04:08.809 --> 00:04:13.677
It copied itself, set up the copy, swapped in ls, and waited for the news.

65
00:04:13.933 --> 00:04:20.105
The gap between fork and exec is where all the setup lives, so no call ever needs a setting for it.

66
00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:21.680
One quick check.

67
00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.453
Suppose the child called exec first, and pointed descriptor 1 at the file after it.

68
00:04:27.453 --> 00:04:29.123
Where would the list go?

69
00:04:29.123 --> 00:04:30.236
Pause and think.

70
00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:34.523
To the terminal.

71
00:04:34.523 --> 00:04:39.699
After a successful exec, the shell's code is gone, so that setup never runs.

72
00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:41.197
Setup goes in the gap.
